AB  - This is a simulation study created to provide insights into using deep learning models to improve Vehicle-To-Everything (V2X) communications security by classifying the types of messages transmitted or received. The dataset is provided in the IEEE Data Port, pre-processed by one-hot encoding, tokenizer, post-padding, and embedding layer. After text preprocessing, the dense vectorize values are inputted into these four deep learning algorithms: Bidirectional Recurrent Neural Network (BRNN), Convolutional – Bidirectional Recurrent Neural Network (CNN-BRNN), Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BLSTM), and Convolutional Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-BLSTM). Researchers use the TensorFlow and Keras python libraries using their default parameters of these models. A flatten and dense layer with a Softmax activation function is also used in its output layer for multilabel classification. The results of the classification metrics observed that the CNN-BRNN produced a superior performance than the rest of the models using the V2X Dataset, having an accuracy of 90%, precision of 98.46%, recall of 80.12%, f1-score of 84.49%, and loss value of 0.45. It has also been observed that BRNN has a better performance than BLSTM models with or without Convolutional Layers.
ER  - 

