AB  - In public safety applications, drones and autonomous aircraft are frequently deployed for mission-critical tasks. Ensuring the integrity and trustworthiness of their operational software-particularly during updates-is essential to maintaining system reliability and safety. Traditional centralized update mechanisms present significant vulnerabilities, including inefficiency, susceptibility to single points of failure, and security breaches, rendering them inadequate for such highstakes environments. To address these challenges, we propose a decentralized, peer-to-peer (P2P) update framework that incorporates a Merkle hash tree-based protocol for integrity verification. This approach not only ensures the authenticity and integrity of software updates but also enhances system resilience by eliminating reliance on centralized infrastructure. We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of our method, comparing it against conventional centralized update architectures. Our findings demonstrate that the proposed solution offers superior scalability, robustness, and security. This research contributes a novel, decentralized update mechanism tailored for autonomous systems, underpinned by cryptographic integrity guarantees provided by the Merkle tree structure.
ER  - 

